suffix.-енный:
===Etymology 1===
{{bor+|ru|cu|-еньнъ}}, from {{m|cu|енъ}} + {{m|cu|ьнъ}}. Originally used to form adjectives from past passive participles in Old Church Slavonic, later reinterpreted as a past passive participle suffix within the Russian literary language. Compare the native past participle suffix {{doublet|ru|-еный|notext=1}}, which developed into an adjective suffix. For the same development compare {{m|ru|-ячий}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|ru|-ённый||when stressed}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{ru-IPA|gem=opt}}

====Suffix====
{{head|ru|suffix|cat2=adjective-forming suffixes}}

# {{n-g|used to form past passive participles from verbs in {{m|ru|-ить}} and {{m|ru|-еть}}, which are frequently [[lexicalize]]d into adjectives}}
#: {{suffixusex|ru|просла́вить|t1=to [[celebrate]]|просла́вленный|t2=[[celebrated]]}}

=====Usage notes=====
* As a means of forming past passive participles, the suffix as written occurs only unstressed; when stressed, it assumes the form {{m|ru|-ённый}}. Generally, this suffix occurs with accent-a verbs (stem-stressed verbs ending in unstressed {{m|ru|-ить}} and {{m|ru|-еть}}) and accent-c verbs (ending-stressed verbs in {{m|ru|-и́ть}} and {{m|ru|-е́ть}}, where the present and synthetic future tense, except for the first person singular, are stem-stressed, for example {{m|ru|води́ть}} with present tense {{m|ru|вожу́}}, {{m|ru|во́дишь}}, {{m|ru|во́дит}}, etc.). It does not normally occur with accent-b verbs (ending-stressed verbs where the entire present and synthetic future tense is ending-stressed, for example {{m|ru|вини́ть}} with present tense {{m|ru|виню́}}, {{m|ru|вини́шь}}, {{m|ru|вини́т}}, etc.), which instead use {{m|ru|-ённый}}. However, there are exceptions in both directions, e.g. accent-c {{m|ru|дели́ть}} (present tense {{m|ru|делю́}}, {{m|ru|де́лишь}}) with past passive participle {{m|ru|делённый}} and accent-b {{m|ru|крои́ть}} (present tense {{m|ru|крою́}}, {{m|ru|крои́шь}}) with past passive participle {{m|ru|кро́енный}}.
* This suffix triggers {{glossary|iotation}} (unlike the homophonous adjective-forming suffix below).

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|ru|-анный}}
* {{l|ru|-янный}}
* {{l|ru|-нный}}

=====See also=====
* {{l|ru|-тый}}

===Etymology 2===

====Pronunciation====
* {{ru-IPA|gem=opt|ann=y}}
* {{ru-IPA|-е́нный|gem=y|ann=y}}

====Suffix====
{{head|ru|suffix|head=-енный|head2=-е́нный|cat2=adjective-forming suffixes}}

# {{n-g|used to form adjectives from nouns, with the meaning "having the property of X"}}
#: {{suffixusex|ru|простра́нство|t1=[[space]]|простра́нственный|t2=[[spatial]]}}
#: {{suffixusex|ru|целому́дрие|t1=[[chastity]]|целому́дренный|t2=[[chaste]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|ма́лый|t1=[[small]]|число́|t2=[[number]]|малочи́сленный|t3=[[scanty]], [[few]]}}
# {{n-g|used to form adjectives from other adjectives, with the colloquial meaning "very X"}}
#: {{suffixusex|ru|высо́кий|t1=[[high]], [[tall]]|altsuf=-е́нный|высоче́нный|t2=very [[high]], very [[tall]] {{q|colloquial}}}}
#: {{suffixusex|ru|здоро́вый|t1=[[healthy]]; [[strong]]; [[big]]|altsuf=-е́нный|здорове́нный|t2=very [[strong]], very [[muscular]]; [[huge]], [[enormous]] {{q|colloquial}}}}

=====Usage notes=====
* This suffix triggers the {{glossary|Slavic first palatalization}} of {{glossary|velar}}s, but does not trigger {{glossary|iotation}} (unlike the homophonous past-participle-forming suffix above).
* In the colloquial meaning "very X", the suffix is normally stressed, while in the meaning "having the property of X" it is normally unstressed.

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|ru}}

