suffix.-ить:
===Etymology===
{{etymon|ru|:inh|orv:-ити<ety:inh<sla-pro:*-iti>>}}
{{inh+|ru|orv|-ити}}, from {{inh|ru|sla-pro|*-iti}}. Originally, from Middle Russian, its second person singular form ({{m|ru|-ишь}}) was always stressed, but in modern Russian is usually non-stressed in transitive verbs. Some exceptions are {{m|ru|прости́шь}}, {{m|ru|роди́шь}}, {{m|ru|сохрани́шь}} etc., especially transitional verbs of Old Church Slavonic origin. This change happened mostly around 1600-1700-s, especially in Pskov, Oryol and Smolensk area, under influence from Belarus and Ukraine, where this process has started somewhat earlier.

===Pronunciation===
* {{q|unstressed}} {{ru-IPA}}
* {{q|stressed}} {{ru-IPA|-и́ть}}

===Suffix===
{{head|ru|suffix|head=-и́ть|head2=-ить|cat2=verb-forming suffixes}}

# {{non-gloss|the infinitive suffix for many verbs; usually indicates a perfective verb}}
# {{non-gloss|a suffix used to form {{glossary|factitive}} verbs from nouns, i.e. verbs having the meaning "to make X(er)" for X an adjective or noun; usually used with a prefix such as {{m|ru|о-}}, {{m|ru|об-}} or {{m|ru|у-}}}}
#: {{sufex|ru|о-|сла́бый|t2=[[weak]]|осла́бить|t3=to [[weaken]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|о-|бе́дный|t2=[[poor]]|!-и́ть|обедни́ть|t4=to [[impoverish]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|о-|свободный|alt2=свобо́д(ный)|t2=[[free]]|!-и́ть|освободи́ть|t4=to [[liberate]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|о-|кайма́|t2=[[border]], [[hem]], [[edge]]|!-и́ть|окайми́ть|t4=to provide with a [[border]], [[hem]] or [[edge]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|о-|зо́лото|t2=[[gold]]|!-и́ть|озолоти́ть|t4=to [[gild]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|об-|о́бщий|t2=[[general]]|!-и́ть|обобщи́ть|t4=to [[generalize]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|у-|то́нкий|t2=[[thin]], [[subtle]], [[fine]]|!-и́ть|утончи́ть|t4=to make (more) [[subtle]], to [[refine]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|у-|крепкий|alt2=кре́п(кий)|t2=[[strong]]|!-и́ть|укрепи́ть|t4=to [[strengthen]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|у-|лу́чший|t2=[[better]]|улу́чшить|t3=to [[improve]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|по-|низкий|alt2=ни́з(кий)|t2=[[low]]|пони́зить|t3=to [[lower]]}}
# {{non-gloss|A suffix used to form negative {{glossary|factitive}} verbs from {{m|ru|без-||[[without]], [[-less]]}} (or {{m|ru|бес-}}) + a noun, i.e. verbs having the meaning "to deprive of X" for X a noun. Usually used with {{m|ru|о-}} and commonly corresponding to English verbs in {{m|en|de-}} or {{m|en|dis-}}.}}
#: {{sufex|ru|о-|без-|лес|t3=[[forest]]|обезле́сить|t4=to [[deforest]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|о-|без-|лю́ди|t3=[[people]]|обезлю́дить|t4=to [[depopulate]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|о-|без-|ору́жие|t3=[[weapon]]|обезору́жить|t4=to [[disarm]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|о-|без-|вред|t3=[[harm]]|обезвре́дить|t4=to [[defuse]], to [[render]] [[harmless]]}}
#: {{sufex|ru|о-|бес-|цвет|t3=[[color]]|обесцве́тить|t4=to [[desaturate]], to [[bleach]], to [[turn]] [[black and white]]}}

====Usage notes====
* When added onto a stem ending in a {{glossary|velar}} consonant, the {{glossary|Slavic first palatalization}} applies.
* When added onto an adjective ending in a suffix such as {{m|ru|-ный}} or {{m|ru|-кий}}, the suffix may or may not be preserved (see examples above for both cases).
* Negative factitives are not always easily distinguished from positive factitives formed from adjectives beginning with {{m|ru|без-||[[-less]]}}; compare {{m|ru|обезвре́дить||to [[render]] [[harmless]]; to [[defuse]] (a [[bomb]])}}, derivable either from {{m|ru|о-}} + {{m|ru|безвредный|безвре́д(ный)|[[harmless]]}} + {{m|ru|-ить}} or from {{m|ru|о-}} + {{m|ru|без-}} + {{m|ru|вред||[[harm]]}} + {{m|ru|-ить}}. (Similarly, the other examples above are alternatively derivable from {{m|ru|безле́сный||[[treeless]]}}, {{m|ru|безлю́дный||[[uninhabited]]}}, {{m|ru|безору́жный||[[unarmed]]}} and {{m|ru|бесцве́тный||[[colorless]]}}, respectively.) Some examples are more easily derived one way than the other; e.g. {{m|ru|обессме́ртить||to [[immortalize]]}} is probably better derived as a positive factitive from {{m|ru|бессме́ртный||[[immortal]]}}.
* Negative factitives, as well as positive factitives derived from adjectives beginning with {{m|ru|без-}} or {{m|ru|бес-}}, have some special properties compared with other factitives. They are normally derived specifically with the prefix {{m|ru|о-}} and the unstressed suffix {{m|ru|-ить}}, form abstract nouns in unstressed {{m|ru|-ение}} (when {{m|ru|-е́ние}} would normally be expected), and form imperfectives in {{m|ru|-ивать}} (rather than {{m|ru|-я́ть}}).

